    q(next_id(), "Medium", "The Philippines' geographic location makes it one of the most disaster-prone countries in the world. An average of 20 typhoons enter Philippine territory annually, and the country sits atop multiple fault lines and active volcanoes. Rather than viewing this as purely a disadvantage, disaster management experts argue that the Philippines has developed world-class expertise in community-based disaster preparedness precisely because of this constant exposure to natural hazards.", "What unexpected argument do disaster management experts make?", ["The Philippines should relocate its population.", "Constant disaster exposure has made the Philippines a leader in community-based preparedness.", "Natural disasters are beneficial for the economy.", "The Philippines experiences fewer disasters than reported."], "Constant disaster exposure has made the Philippines a leader in community-based preparedness.", "The passage states experts argue the Philippines 'has developed world-class expertise in community-based disaster preparedness precisely because of this constant exposure.'", ["reading comprehension", "inference"]),
    q(next_id(), "Medium", "Impostor syndrome is the persistent feeling of being a fraud despite evidence of competence and achievement. People experiencing this phenomenon attribute their success to luck or external factors rather than their own abilities. It is particularly common among high-achieving individuals entering new roles or environments, and can lead to anxiety, overwork, and reluctance to pursue opportunities.", "Who is particularly affected by impostor syndrome?", ["People who are genuinely incompetent.", "High-achieving individuals entering new roles or environments.", "Only students and young professionals.", "People who never succeed."], "High-achieving individuals entering new roles or environments.", "The passage states impostor syndrome 'is particularly common among high-achieving individuals entering new roles or environments.'", ["reading comprehension", "supporting details"]),
    q(next_id(), "Medium", "The tragedy of the commons describes a situation where individuals acting in their own self-interest deplete a shared resource, even though it is in no one's long-term interest for the resource to be destroyed. Overfishing in Philippine waters illustrates this concept: each fisher catches as much as possible to maximize personal income, but collectively this behavior depletes fish stocks to unsustainable levels.", "What does the tragedy of the commons describe?", ["Government failure to provide resources.", "Individual self-interest depleting shared resources against everyone's long-term interest.", "The privatization of public land.", "Competition between businesses."], "Individual self-interest depleting shared resources against everyone's long-term interest.", "The passage defines the tragedy of the commons as individuals acting in self-interest depleting a shared resource against everyone's long-term interest.", ["reading comprehension", "main idea"]),
    q(next_id(), "Medium", "Mindfulness meditation involves paying deliberate attention to the present moment without judgment. Clinical studies have demonstrated its effectiveness in reducing symptoms of anxiety, depression, and chronic pain. Unlike medication, mindfulness has no side effects and can be practiced anywhere without cost. However, researchers caution that it is not a replacement for professional mental health treatment in severe cases.", "What limitation of mindfulness does the passage acknowledge?", ["It is too expensive for most people.", "It is not a replacement for professional treatment in severe cases.", "It only works for physical pain.", "It requires years of practice to be effective."], "It is not a replacement for professional treatment in severe cases.", "The passage states researchers caution that mindfulness 'is not a replacement for professional mental health treatment in severe cases.'", ["reading comprehension", "supporting details"]),
    q(next_id(), "Medium", "The anchoring effect is a cognitive bias where people rely too heavily on the first piece of information they encounter when making decisions. In salary negotiations, for example, the first number mentioned tends to anchor the entire discussion, regardless of whether that number is reasonable. Skilled negotiators exploit this by making an ambitious opening offer, knowing it will pull the final agreement in their direction.", "How do skilled negotiators exploit the anchoring effect?", ["By refusing to name any number.", "By making an ambitious opening offer to pull the final agreement their way.", "By always accepting the first offer.", "By researching the other party's salary history."], "By making an ambitious opening offer to pull the final agreement their way.", "The passage states skilled negotiators 'exploit this by making an ambitious opening offer, knowing it will pull the final agreement in their direction.'", ["reading comprehension", "supporting details"]),
    q(next_id(), "Medium", "Food deserts are areas where residents have limited access to affordable, nutritious food, typically because there are no grocery stores or fresh food markets within reasonable distance. In the Philippines, food deserts exist in both remote rural areas and dense urban slums. Residents of these areas often rely on convenience stores and fast food outlets, contributing to poor nutrition and diet-related health problems.", "What is a food desert?", ["An area with too many restaurants.", "An area with limited access to affordable, nutritious food.", "A desert region where no food grows.", "An area where food is wasted."], "An area with limited access to affordable, nutritious food.", "The passage defines food deserts as 'areas where residents have limited access to affordable, nutritious food.'", ["reading comprehension", "supporting details"]),
